Claude Code Instructions

SSF is an embedded toolkit.
SSF shall use the functionality of other SSF modules before writing ad-hoc code for SSF.
SSF shall use standard C library functions only if SSF does not have a replacement for it.
SSF should generally prefer fixed or stack memory over dynamic memory allocations.
SSF shall follow all applicable RFCs and best practices when applicable.
SSF is expected to work on Big and Little Endian systems.
SSF may run on 8, 16, 32, or 64-bit microcontrollers.
SSF generally executes in a single threaded context, but can be used on multi-threaded systems.
SSF SHALL NOT BLOCK.
SSF may use the Low Priority Task Queue to offload time-consuming operations so that it does not block the operation of the stack.
SSF modules SHALL NOT BE MODIFIED without explicit approval.
SSF module use DBC. All input parameters shall be checked against contact as appropriate.


SSF shall following the coding and documentation style:
- Function header comments in a block, shall be limited to 1 line of comment.
- Non-trivial conditional statements shall be preceeded by a question that makes clear the purpose, the true clause comment shall start with 'Yes, ...', and the false clause commane shall start with 'No, ...'.
- Other inline comments shall be limited to 1 line with not extra end of line padding.
- ALL functions shall have comprehensive DBC assertions to check function parameters and invariants.
- Initialization functions shall track module level or context level that it has been initialized before use.
- Functions that may fails shall return a bool. Functions that can't fail shall return a void. Functions that can't fail may return a single value. Functions with multiple outputs shall return void.
- Function arguments that are C string shall end with 'Str'.
- Function arguments that are optional shall end with 'Opt'.
- Function argument that indicate the size of a previous argument shall end with 'Size'
- Function arguments that the lenght of a previous argument shall end with 'Len'.
- Buffers that have a variable size must pass the size along with the buffer pointer.
- Multi-line comments that may be useful to callers of the API shall be put into the documentation file not the source code.
- Non printable characters are not permitted in source or comments within any .c or .h file.
- When making incremental changes to any module, they will made using a TDD (Test Driven Development) sequence.
- No tabs, whitespace only, default 4 indent.
